findings.body:
The six new `public static let` accessors in `Strings.swift` (`StudiesSettingTitleV3`, `StudiesSettingLinkV3`, `StudiesSettingMessageV3`, `RolloutsSettingTitle`, `RolloutsSettingLink`, `RolloutsSettingMessage`) have no callers in the repo. The same is true of the Focus constants (`labelStudiesV2`, `detailTextStudiesV2`, `labelRollouts`, `detailTextRollouts`, `detailTextSendUsageDataV2`).

The PR description says this is a base for a later split of the Studies toggle, so this may be intentional. Even so, unused accessors can't be checked by the compiler for correctness of the key-to-value mapping, and they will drift from the xliff if the copy changes before they're wired up. Consider moving them into the follow-up PR that uses them, or noting in the PR that they are intentionally pre-staged.

::diff{file="firefox-ios/Shared/Strings.swift" lines="6232-6261"}

This PR's title and description are about firefox-ios Settings strings, but `focus-ios/Blockzilla/UIComponents/UIConstants.swift` is a separate app with its own string tables and release cycle. Adding Focus keys here mixes two products into one change and makes it harder to review each app's copy and translation pipeline on its own.

If the Focus strings are needed, they should be in a Focus-scoped PR so that Focus l10n reviewers can check them. If this is an intentional shared rollout, the PR description should say so.

::diff{file="focus-ios/Blockzilla/UIComponents/UIConstants.swift" lines="417-431"}
